=B. It reveals the narrator's blind spot about control.#Correct. Repetition exposes self-deception. This answer matches the lesson skill and is supported by the specific creative-writing evidence in the prompt.
~C. It removes character perspective.#Incorrect. It reveals perspective. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~D. It explains grammar.#Incorrect. It is craft purpose. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U03_L07_Q16::[MLA.CW.FIC.07] Central idea: memory can guide a person forward. Which adapted object fits best?{
~A. An unrelated parking ticket.#Incorrect. It does not connect by itself. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~B. A random pencil with no role.#Incorrect. It lacks meaning. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~C. A copied magical object with no transformation.#Incorrect. Transformation is needed. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=D. A grandmother's recipe card used in a new city.#Correct. The object carries memory into new action.
}

::CW_U03_L07_Q17::[MLA.CW.FIC.06] A student's scene has theme, conflict, and growth, but no evidence in the explanation. What should be added?{
~A. More vague praise.#Incorrect. Vague praise is not evidence. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~B. A completely unrelated summary.#Incorrect. It does not support the explanation. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=C. Specific passage details showing how the elements develop the idea.#Correct. Evidence supports analysis. This answer matches the lesson skill and is supported by the specific creative-writing evidence in the prompt.
~D. No details at all.#Incorrect. Evidence is required. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U03_L07_Q18::[MLA.CW.FIC.07] Passage: The old key no longer opens the house, but the character keeps it while choosing a new apartment. What central idea might it support?{
=A. Leaving can include carrying memory forward.#Correct. The key connects past and change. This answer matches the lesson skill and is supported by the specific creative-writing evidence in the prompt.
~B. Keys are useless in every story.#Incorrect. The key has symbolic value. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~C. Apartments cannot have themes.#Incorrect. Setting can support theme. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~D. Memory prevents all change.#Incorrect. The character still chooses a new apartment.
}

::CW_U03_L07_Q19::[MLA.CW.FIC.06] Which plan best shows conflicting perspectives?{
~A. Both characters agree without tension.#Incorrect. No conflict. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=B. One character sees moving as betrayal, while another sees it as survival.#Correct. The same choice has different meanings. This answer matches the lesson skill and is supported by the specific creative-writing evidence in the prompt.
~C. A character names every object in a room.#Incorrect. Listing is not perspective conflict. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~D. No character has an opinion.#Incorrect. Perspective requires viewpoint. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U03_L07_Q20::[MLA.CW.FIC.07] What makes an adaptation purposeful in integrated fiction?{
~A. It changes only font.#Incorrect. Font is not enough. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~B. It copies everything.#Incorrect. Adaptation transforms. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~C. It has no reason.#Incorrect. Purpose is required. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=D. The transformed source element affects theme, conflict, or character growth.#Correct. It must matter to the scene. This answer matches the lesson skill and is supported by the specific creative-writing evidence in the prompt.
}

::CW_U03_L07_Q21::[MLA.CW.FIC.06] Scene plan: A character wants independence, receives a mentor's marked-up draft, resists feedback, then revises successfully. What theme is strongest?{
=A. Growth can require accepting guidance.#Correct. The mentor object and revision arc support the theme.
~B. Feedback always ruins writing.#Incorrect. Feedback helps. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~C. Independence means ignoring everyone.#Incorrect. The character changes from that belief. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~D. Drafts have no meaning.#Incorrect. The draft is central. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U03_L07_Q22::[MLA.CW.FIC.07] Which response best explains integration?{
~A. The story uses many names.#Incorrect. Names alone do not integrate meaning. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~B. The writer avoids revision.#Incorrect. Integration often requires revision. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=C. The source object, central idea, and character change all point to the same reader effect.#Correct. This defines integrated fiction. This answer matches the lesson skill and is supported by the specific creative-writing evidence in the prompt.
~D. The setting is long.#Incorrect. Length does not prove integration. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U03_L07_Q23::[MLA.CW.FIC.06] Passage: A character says courage means never fearing anything, then speaks while her hands shake. What changed understanding appears?{
~A. Courage means silence.#Incorrect. She speaks. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=B. Courage can exist while fear is still present.#Correct. The action revises her belief. This answer matches the lesson skill and is supported by the specific creative-writing evidence in the prompt.
~C. Fear stops all action.#Incorrect. She acts. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~D. Hands are the theme.#Incorrect. Shaking hands are evidence. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U03_L07_Q24::[MLA.CW.FIC.07] A scene has a strong central idea but an unrelated comic side plot that takes half the scene. What revision is best?{
~A. Add more unrelated comedy.#Incorrect. This worsens the issue. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~B. Remove the central idea.#Incorrect. It guides the scene. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=C. Cut or revise the side plot so it supports the central idea.#Correct. Integration requires focus. This answer matches the lesson skill and is supported by the specific creative-writing evidence in the prompt.
~D. Keep everything because length equals quality.#Incorrect. Length is not integration. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U03_L07_Q25::[MLA.CW.FIC.06] Which final check best prepares an integrated fiction scene?{
~A. Did I add details randomly?#Incorrect. Random details weaken integration. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~B. Did I remove all character change?#Incorrect. Character growth matters. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~C. Did I ignore evidence?#Incorrect. Evidence is required. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=D. Do theme, source influence, central idea, purpose, conflict, and growth support the same effect?#Correct. This checks Unit 3 integration. This answer matches the lesson skill and is supported by the specific creative-writing evidence in the prompt.
}
